  "number": "3.1",
  "title": "Electric Field and Electric Force",
  "body": " Electric Field and Electric Force  Watch on x1.25   The electric force is the force on a particle of charge from the electric force. It's applicable even if charge is moving. This means it's more general than Coulomb's Law.  When calculating the contribution from source charges to at a point, you can sum the contribution of each charge individually, using vector addition.  Recall the electrostatic force on particle 2 from particle 1 is Then the electric field caused by particle 1 is given by  Since particle 2 has been removed from the equation, the subscript 2 is just the point we're evaluating the electric field at. This makes sense because if particle 2 where located at that point, then it would experience the force at that location from particle 1.  "
