  "body": "    Find the charge density of the following uniformly charged object. Also, say whether it is naturally a 1-D, 2-D, or 3-D density (you can do this by using the appropriate density symbol: , , or ).  Object: A solid sphere of radius and total charge .    Calculate the formula for the amount of charge (the charge element), in terms of the density you got in Part one, and appropriate coordinate variables. Draw a picture showing your within the object.  Object: A solid sphere of radius and total charge is sliced into disk-shaped charge elements. The slices are parallel to the x-y plane. (This one is more complex; since the charge elements are of different radii, a formula for the radius as a function of the slice's coordinate position will need to appear in the answer.)    Calculate the formula for the distance between the center of a general (i.e., not in a special spot like the ends or middle) charge element (slice) and the point where we are placing an imaginary test charge to measure the electric field. The formula should be in terms of the appropriate coordinate variables.  Object: A solid sphere of radius and total charge is sliced into disk-shaped charge elements. The slices are parallel to the x-y plane. The sphere is centered at the origin. is at .     "
