  "number": "10.2",
  "title": "Chapter Exercises",
  "body": " Chapter Exercises   Method A - Equivalent Resistance Method     replacement tag   replacement tag    a) Find the voltages and currents of all the resistors in the following circuit.  b) Let's think about how the waterfall analogy applies to a map of a circuit. In the above circuit, there are some different ways to travel between the bottom (point a) and the top (point b) of the battery. For the circuit above, assume point a is grounded, which means we assume it has 0V potential, like the altitude of the bottom of a waterfall. Considering the potential differences you previously calculated for all the resistors (which are like the amount of altitude change when you pass through each resistor, pretending each resistor is like a separate small waterfall), what must be the potentials, in Volts, at locations f, e, d, c, and b? Note that there is no voltage\/altitude change while travelling along a section of plain wire with no resistor. (Hint: if you did it right, then no matter whether you add up the changes along path a-f-e-c-b or along path a-f-e-d-c-b, you should find that point b winds up with a potential of 12V.)      The following resistor network is hooked up with a 12.0V battery between terminal A and terminal B. Find the voltages and currents of all the resistors.   replacement tag   replacement tag        For the following circuit, assume a 9.00V battery is hooked up between terminals A and B. Assuming all values are good to 3 sig. figs., find the voltage and current of resistor .   replacement tag   replacement tag        Method B - Kirchhoff's Rules Method    Solve the voltages and currents of the resistors in this circuit:   replacement tag   replacement tag        Solve the voltages and currents of the resistors in this circuit:   replacement tag   replacement tag        Solve the voltages and currents of the resistors in this circuit:   replacement tag   replacement tag        Sometimes, the voltage of a battery is designated by the symbol . Solve for the voltage of the third battery, , in this circuit (note the current is already given):   replacement tag   replacement tag        Method C - Thevenin's Theorem Method    Thévenin’s Theorem actually works for simplifying a circuit no matter what kind of device you put as the load, since you remove the load while calculating the Thévenin equivalent values. For example, in the following circuit, we have inserted a capacitor as the load device instead of a resistor! But it doesn’t matter for applying the theorem. Calculate the Thévenin equivalent voltage and Thévenin equivalent resistance, then redraw the circuit in its Thévenin simplified form.   replacement tag   replacement tag        In the following circuit, suppose the load resistor is . (While Kirchhoff’s Laws could be used to solve this circuit, Thévenin’s Theorem provides another way, which is particularly efficient if a few different load values will be tried.)   replacement tag   replacement tag    a) Assuming is the load, calculate the Thévenin equivalent voltage and Thévenin equivalent resistance, then redraw the circuit in its Thévenin simplified form. Then, use the simplified circuit to calculate the current in the 40.0 load.  b) Use your simplified circuit to recalculate if were replaced with: i) a 50.0 resistor, ii) a 60.0 resistor, iii) a 70.0 resistor.     "
